1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,050
Listen to a talk in a literature class.

2
00:00:04,050 --> 00:00:12,280
Magical realism is a style of fiction that combines realistic narrative with surreal elements of magic and fantasy.

3
00:00:12,280 --> 00:00:18,050
Unlike pure fantasy, magical realism is set in a realistic world.

4
00:00:18,050 --> 00:00:23,250
But extraordinary events occur without explanation, blending the ordinary with the extraordinary.

5
00:00:23,250 --> 00:00:30,542
One of the most famous examples of magical realism is Gabriel García Márquez's novel

6
00:00:30,542 --> 00:00:32,009
"One Hundred Years of Solitude."

7
00:00:32,009 --> 00:00:39,010
In this book, everyday life in the fictional town of Macondo is interwoven with fantastic occurrences

8
00:00:39,010 --> 00:00:42,210
such as a character ascending to the heavens while hanging out laundry.

9
00:00:42,210 --> 00:00:46,862
These magical events are presented in a matter-of-fact manner, as if this is the

10
00:00:46,862 --> 00:00:48,010
way things should be.

11
00:00:48,010 --> 00:00:50,080
And this is a hallmark of the genre.

12
00:00:50,080 --> 00:00:56,165
Magical realism often serves to highlight complex social and cultural issues, offering a unique

13
00:00:56,165 --> 00:01:00,000
way to explore themes like identity, history and politics.

14
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:04,880
The blending of reality and magic allows authors to delve into deeper truths

15
00:01:04,880 --> 00:01:07,250
and challenge readers' perceptions of the world.

16
00:01:07,250 --> 00:01:13,110
Next, we will examine how magical realism has influenced other literary traditions

17
00:01:13,110 --> 00:01:16,100
and its role in contemporary literature.
